composite a clear cart's layers once instead of three times a frame

A clear cart is drawn as three sprites over the same rect: the game-coloured
interior, the board, then the plastic and its label. On a device with no GPU
that is three full-size blends of a 312x337 texture, two of which touch nearly
every pixel. Measured on the handheld:

    tex id 9  278x300  6.0 ms  72 ns/px -- skip 5082  copy 0     blend 78318
    tex id 36 278x300  2.1 ms  25 ns/px -- skip 18214 copy 62516 blend 2670
    tex id 6  278x300  4.2 ms  50 ns/px -- skip 5272  copy 35046 blend 43082

13 ms for one cart, against 2 ms for a solid one, and three clear shells in
view put the shelf at 30-41 ms a frame.

The three layers never move relative to each other, so they are composited
into the face once, on the worker thread that already builds it, and the shelf
sends one sprite. After, on the same shelf:

    sprites 25.6-37.3 ms over 18-20 draws  ->  15.4-16.0 over 14
    whole frame ~30-41 ms, worst 44-78     ->  ~19-20 ms, worst 21-27
    one clear cart 13 ms                   ->  1.9 ms, mostly a straight copy

It also drops a full-size texture per clear cart from memory, which on a
handheld with 101 MB is worth having on its own.

The cost, which is a real difference and not a free win: a faded cart -- the
ones either side of the selection -- now composites as one stack at that
alpha, rather than layer over layer each at that alpha. Upstream's way darkens
twice where the layers overlap and this does not. It is arguably the more
correct of the two, and it is certainly not the same pixels, which is why the
header says so and why it was looked at on the handheld before being kept.

`a_clear_cart_flattens_to_what_its_three_layers_drew` composites the layers
independently and checks the flattened face against them; the shelf keeps the
three-draw path for any cart that still has an interior of its own.

Upstream added `a_clear_cart_is_drawn_over_its_board_and_a_solid_one_is_not`
after this was written, which asserts the board arrives as its own layer. That
is exactly what this stops doing, so the assertion is turned around here: the
board must *not* be drawn separately, because the face already carries it.
`cart.rs` is the test that says the flattened face is right, by compositing
the three layers independently and comparing.

